findings.message:
In mov_read_iloc (and identically in mov_read_infe), the code searches the c->heif_item[] array for an entry whose item_id matches the one just parsed from the file, allocating a new HEIFItem into the first NULL slot if one is found along the way:

    for (int j = 0; j < c->nb_heif_item; j++) {
        item = c->heif_item[j];
        if (!item)
            item = c->heif_item[j] = av_mallocz(sizeof(*item));
        else if (item->item_id != item_id)
            continue;
        break;
    }
    if (!item)
        return AVERROR(ENOMEM);

    item->item_id = item_id;

The loop terminates in three distinct cases that are NOT distinguished afterward: (1) a NULL slot was found and freshly allocated (correct - a new item), (2) an existing slot whose item_id matches was found (correct - the intended item), or (3) the loop simply ran off the end of the array (j == c->nb_heif_item) without ever finding a NULL slot or a matching id, leaving `item` pointing at the LAST array entry visited, which belongs to an entirely different, already-populated item.

Only a `!item` (allocation failure) check follows the loop; there is no check that `j < c->nb_heif_item`, i.e. no check that the search actually succeeded. When case (3) occurs, the code proceeds to call `item->item_id = item_id;` and continue populating fields, silently corrupting/overwriting a pre-existing, unrelated HEIFItem structure that had already been fully initialized and possibly already had a stream created from it (via heif_add_stream). This state confusion between HEIFItem objects and AVStream/codec parameters led to an assertion failure downstream (`Assertion ref failed at src/libavformat/mov.c:10649`) discovered by OSS-Fuzz, and more generally is a type/identity-confusion bug that can corrupt demuxer state when processing a crafted/fuzzed MOV/HEIF file with iloc/iinf boxes whose item_id lists don't align as expected.

